<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP, permitted deliberately.

  Since API 28 the platform blocks cleartext by default, and it had never come up in this
  app: MQTT is a raw socket (the platform cannot see it as HTTP) and RTSP is not HTTP
  either. The camera snapshot is the first real HTTP request the app makes, and it goes to
  a homelab camera at something like http://192.168.1.60/snapshot.jpg. Without this the
  fetch fails with "CLEARTEXT communication to <host> not permitted" while the exact same
  URL works in curl on the laptop — see docs/10.

  This cannot be scoped to a domain list. Every address in this app is typed by the user at
  runtime; there is no compile-time host to allowlist, by deliberate design (a published APK
  that knows a gate's address is a liability — see CLAUDE.md). The exposure is bounded by
  what the app actually sends over HTTP: one GET to a user-supplied camera address, with
  credentials the same user typed, over a VPN they control. Gate commands do not travel this
  way — they are MQTT, and TLS for those is a separate switch in Settings.

  If the camera ever gets a certificate, prefer https:// in the snapshot URL: this file
  permits cleartext, it does not require it.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"/>
</network-security-config>
